hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in his treatise  on human nature in book two part three  section three David Hume will bring up  this conception of calm passions which  sounds to us in the present very much  like an oxymoron but plays an important  role in his moral philosophy  particularly in his account of human  action and what makes our  decision-making processes actually work  or how the will is determined and Hume  has already argued by the point that he  brings this up that reason by itself  doesn't actually oppose passions so if  we're feeling a strong passion like fear  or desire or anger or hope or joy  sadness grief pick whatever you want  reason by itself cannot actually oppose  these so what then can well one thing  that can oppose a passion is another  passion and Hume is going to tell us  that not only do we have violent  passions the things that we typically  understand as our human emotions desires  drives but that we also have these what  he's calling calm passion  or as he'll to use other language that  he uses in this section desires or  tendencies he also talks about them as  actions of the mind as well and if we  think about what these are they're  coming from as he says the same faculty  as the other passions they're not coming  from the faculty that deals with truth  and falsity they're not coming from the  understanding or reason they are coming  from our faculty of effectivity or  however you want to label it and it  tells us that these these come in two  main ways  he says there's certain column desires  and tendencies which though they be real  passions produce little emotion in the  mind and are known more by their effects  than their immediate feeling or  sensation they're of two kinds either  here's the first kind certain instincts  originally implanted in our natures so  these are these are the things that you  can count on every single human being  having unless there's some sort of  deficiency of human nature within that  person and it gives us what we shouldn't  take to be an exhaustive list but it  gives us some great examples I said such  as benevolence this this desire to help  other people to do good to them he also  calls this sympathy at other places and  here's another example resentment that  is when we feel like we're being treated  in a way that we don't like we want to  retaliate or cause the other person some  pain or humiliation some lowering of  their status other examples of this he  says are the love of life this constant  desire to continue in existence and to  do the things that will actually augment  our existence and to ward off the things  that will will impede it and then he has  a very specific example kindness to  children this is something that he seems  to think is implanted within us and when  we might extend this not only to  children but even to you know animal  little children of animals you know like  hit ins and puppies or anything like  that that senses oh look how cute that  kitten is right humans say that's  actually a calm passion that we feel so  these are things that can oppose other  passions then he says there's another  class of comp ashens and it calls this  the general appetite to good and  aversion to evil considered merely as  such so considered without any  particular object but just considered in  general and this is something that you  know it plays a major role within human  moral psychology and his ethics but I  think also you can see playing a role in  many other people's moral philosophy as  well particularly this general appetite  it's a good and aversion to evil which  is the basis of for example natural law  theory right so humans saying that we  all have these unless there's been some  sort of damage to our human nature in  some way we all have these operative  within ourselves churning away going  without us feeling them as strong  emotions but they can sort of keep us on  track and they can oppose other passions  now he says that these are easily  confused with the determinations of  reason now why would that be why would  these calm passions coming from a  different source than rationality be  confused with rationality he says well  reason itself doesn't produce any  sensible emotion so it proceeds that  every action of the mind which operates  with the same calmness and tranquillity  is confounded with reason by all those  who judge of things from the first view  and appearance which would include not  only ordinary people but in his view  many other intellectuals and even moral  philosophers they are judging by the  lack of being moved  a violent way that that what the calm  passions are dictating is in fact the  voice of reason he also gives us another  explanation for this as well that  there's a similar sensation going on he  says that whenever any of these passions  are calm and caused no disorder in the  soul they're readily taken for  determinations of reason they're  supposed to proceed from the same  faculty with that which judges of truth  and falsehood their nature and  principles have been supposed the same  because their sensations are not  evidently different they feel the same  to us it's sort of a lack of feeling  however and so humans saying what's  really important here is that we've got  two different faculties in play now he  also tells us that we do have these  violent emotions though the things that  we typically think of as passions or  emotions  he says there's violence emotions of the  same kind which you have a likewise a  great influence on that faculty so he  has in this example when I receive any  injury from another I often feel a  violent passion of resentment which  makes me desire his evil and punishment  independent of all considerations of  pleasure and advantage to myself when I  am immediately threatened with any  grievous ill my fears apprehensions and  aversions rise to a great height and  produce a sensible emotion so this leads  us to be able to oppose those passions  to things like these instincts  originally planted like the desire for  self-preservation or the desire not to  harm you know the innocence the anger or  fear could drive us to ignore that and  whom tells us something really  interesting here about how we ought to  judge human beings morally ethically so  when there's a conflict between these  calm passions that are always operative  within us to some degree and more  immediate violent passions which could  lead us to do other things and  when these are actually in conflict with  each other what decides what determines  us what settles the account and has us  go one way or the other  Hume tells us that there's two main  things that we can look at one is the  general character of the person the  other is the present disposition of the  person so the state that the person is  in at the time perhaps they're stressed  out perhaps they're laboring under some  misapprehensions that could be part of  their general they're there a particular  present disposition but we can also  think about the general character of a  person some people are prone to anger  other people are prone to allowing fear  to drive them to do things that their  other drives or instincts or whatever  you want to call them would say no no  this is not a good idea and to do things  that other people don't seem to fall  into and we can say the same thing about  desires for different pleasures or  aversions grief can make people do all  sorts of things you know the desire to  take care of one's children great  motivator to doing the wrong thing I'm  like you know counts of many people we  could think of the corruption scandal  recently but just think about how you  have to tell parents at sporting events  not to yell at the referees when their  team is losing or at the coach for not  putting their kid in you know those are  examples where disposition would play an  important role but it could also be you  know the present or the disposition or  the general character of that person  would be deciding it so he says what we  call strength of mind implies the  prevalence of the calm passions above  the violence  a person who has presence of mind  typically will follow their calm  passions instead of following the  violent emotions that they're feeling  but he also sells says that we may  easily observe there's no person so  constantly  possessed of this virtue has never on  any occasion to yield to the  solicitations of passion and desire and  so this allows us to understand how it  is that we often do these things that  are not just reasonable but are based in  our human nature without necessarily  having some sort of great effective  emotion at every single point and how it  is that we can have a conflict between  what appears to be reason and appears to  be desire but is really calm passion and  violent passion in conflict with each  other  mediated by general character and  present disposition of the person  you
 

